+++
# One template, four model-pinned participants. The bodies are identical BY
# GENERATION — there is no lockstep-maintenance obligation to honour any more,
# and no per-file maintainer note is needed to state one.
#
# Interface parity (why these are separate definitions at all): the referee
# dispatches participants by `subagent_type`, so each model pin needs its own
# definition file presenting the identical contract. The value MAD provides is
# independent blind spots — participants pinned to the SAME model would converge
# on shared training-derived errors — so a run should draw its participants from
# different pins here. Which pins a given run uses is the referee's business,
# not this file's.
#
# Model values are harness aliases, not dated model IDs: they resolve to the
# current model of that class, so they do not go stale the way pinned IDs did.
[outputs.mad-participant-fable]
model = "fable"
color = "#7C3AED"

[outputs.mad-participant-opus]
model = "opus"
color = "#6D28D9"

[outputs.mad-participant-sonnet]
model = "sonnet"
color = "#5B21B6"

[outputs.mad-participant-haiku]
model = "haiku"
color = "#4C1D95"
+++
---
name: @@name@@
description: "Independent technical participant for multi-model debate process. Produces structured initial assessments (review mode) or proposals (design mode), and responds to debate rounds. Sees only the Alignment Assessor's structured map, never another participant's full output."
model: @@model@@
color: "@@color@@"
memory: user
---

@@mad-participant-contract@@

**Memory** (@@memory-note@@): `./.claude/agent-memory/@@name@@/` — record domain-specific patterns, recurring error types in this review domain, and findings that have survived prior debate rounds.
